A||||11||Sootah||How do I get a loan without a co-signer?||||5||
Z||000000||Sootah||09-25-2001||01:05 AM||||I'm 19, have no previous credit, and nothing of value to use as collateral. Herin lies my problem. How do I go about getting a loan w/o having a co-signer? My mother doesnt want to sign with me, as they may need to get themselves a loan sometime reasonably soon and they are worried about the whole payment thing if for some reason I dump this. I want a Fiero, I'd also like something of a credit history. Any suggestions?<P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/Sootah__Nuclear_Happy_Face_Sig.jpg">  <BR><sup><I>Never pull your pants down in Cosco, the dang monkeys will getcha</I></sup> <BR><sup><B>AIM</B>: KSSouter</sup>||||reg||5
Z||000001||dennis_6||09-25-2001||01:12 AM||||Buy here Pay here places work.<BR>Some big dealers can pull strings.<BR>Either way the interest will suck I know.<BR>On a side note I got a loan from this place called E-Loan on the internet.  I am 22 just got out of college not to long ago and worked various jobs while in college so people kept giving me the time on job junk.  But eloan gave me a good loan at a good interest rate.<p>[This message has been edited by dennis_6 (edited 09-25-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000002||Sootah||09-25-2001||01:46 AM||||Gaaawd, the smallest loan they want to give me is $7500!! I don't need anywhere near that much!! Have any place that will give me about $1500?||||reg||1
Z||000003||olympic||09-25-2001||05:23 AM||||Get a low interest credit card if $1500 is all you need.  Some card companies have a low interest option where you pay an annual fee of like $20 and it cuts your rate to under 10%.  Just keep sending applications to different issuers until one of them gives you one.<P>I don't want to get into a legal argument but all the CC company can verify is your credit history.  So how you fill in the application in regards to your income and assets is "up to you".  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000004||Monkeyman||09-25-2001||05:45 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Sootah:<BR><B>Gaaawd, the smallest loan they want to give me is $7500!! I don't need anywhere near that much!! Have any place that will give me about $1500?</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Take the $7500 and either send the remaining 6 grand back to them (as a payment) or put it in the bank and let it collect interest while you're paying the loan back.  If you deposit it in the bank and pay on the loan for the full amount of time, you'll look better to future lenders and if you ever run into any financial trouble as you're paying off the loan, you'll have most of the money in the bank to draw off of.  It'll cost you a little more this way (your loan interest rate will undoubtedly be more than the interest you'll draw on the savings acct), but just consider that the price you have to pay to get some good credit established.<P>||||reg||1
Z||000005||thomas_l||09-25-2001||10:55 AM||||If you have access to a credit union or small local bank you might be able to get a loan if you pony up a sig down payment. Back in the day, I bought my first nice car a 77 Grand Prix (quit laughing - it wasn't old at the time  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">) for $1,800 = $300 down and $1500 from the local bank.<P>At the time I had no credit, but my folks used that bank and they knew who I was.||||reg||1
Z||000006||rogergarrison||09-25-2001||12:15 PM||||most of the time, your gonna have to do the buy here-pay here lots. You dont have anything for someone to rate your credit. Id suggest getting several credit cards and using them to pay for gas, movie tickets,auto parts,  etc. Pay it off every month and you pay no interest and it will only show that you pay your bill on time all the time. It wont do you any good to get them and not use them. It may take you 6 months to a year to get yourself established. Remember dont just buy stuff because you can charge it, only buy what you would ordinarily and keep that amount in the bank to pay the bill when it comes. Also good because it shows in your record that you have a bank account in good standing.||||reg||1
Z||000007||MinnGreenGT||09-25-2001||01:42 PM||||Do the card thing (buy gas and small crap and pay it off entirely every month).  I did that with two cards- I still have perfect credit and I bought a house 1 1/2 years ago and the mortgage guy said that my income was on the bottom end, but my perfect credit rating pulled me through without problems!  Just DON'T EVER get behind on payments...it'll kill you quick.<P>The idea of the $7500 loan is cool, return the other $6K... just make sure there isn't any penatly for paying off the balance early (I've seen places that do that). Good luck!<P>------------------<BR>   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/grngtsig3.jpg">     <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/fullstaff.gif">   <BR>Medium Metallic Green 87 GT<BR>3.1L High Output<BR>4 Spd OD Tranny<BR>Open (read: Loud) Exhaust<BR>and More!<BR>-Rob Bartlett||||reg||1
Z||000008||Formula||09-25-2001||02:30 PM||||get the 7500 loan and repay with the 6000.  Youll have to pay more in the end but you'll get good credit and have a couple years before you have to start actually paying off the 1500.<P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/Formulasigs.JPG"> ||||reg||1
Z||000009||Butter||09-25-2001||03:07 PM||||Build your credit with some institution that will be around for a while.  The small town bank I've been doing business with most of my life sold out to a large corporation and it cut my open note credit in half per the new corporation's rules.<P>Coarse you could probally get Mr. Smith & Wesson to sign for you!||||reg||12
Z||000010||Red2m4||09-25-2001||11:00 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by olympic:<BR><B><BR>I don't want to get into a legal argument but all the CC company can verify is your credit history.  So how you fill in the application in regards to your income and assets is "up to you".   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Is that true in the US also?<P>||||reg||1
Z||000011||87GTZ34||09-26-2001||10:38 PM||||I like MMs advice.  That is a good way to lower your interest rate for the period of the load and build credit history.  You just need to be disciplined and don't use the money to put in a 3.4, new suspension, exhaust and tires.... GL||||reg||1
